sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 one task system, native Swamp schedules; DECISIONS Schedules (Nick 2026-11-02), R2-R4.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: schedule:run
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: schedule:run 7f3c…<br/>Schedule UUID
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit schedule:run 7f3c9a2e-41d0-4b8a-9c11-2f6e0d5b7a10`
  %%> - syntax: `<uuid> [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the UUID of the Schedule, as `schedule:list` shows it
  %%> Not changed:
  %%> - the Schedule's timing; the next calendar fire stays as it was
  %%> - an enabled Schedule stays enabled, and a disabled one stays disabled
  Note over GW: Admit a task from template<br/>task #5140 · trigger: operator
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42
  %%> - Schedule 7f3c… is active, enabled or disabled
  %%> - Instance 42 is still on Node 12
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5140 recorded from the Schedule's template, with trigger `operator`
  %%> - the same intent a calendar fire gets: user, working directory, shell, timeout and command
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>task-command-run
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one root run of `task-command-run` on serve (Node 209), the workflow each calendar fire of `schedule-7f3c…` runs nested
  %%> Why not `schedule-7f3c…` itself:
  %%> - its inputs are fixed and carry no task number; the task's own run carries it, so the Gateway projects it like any task
  %%> - every root run of `schedule-7f3c…` is then a calendar fire
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  %%> - the user, the working directory, the shell and the timeout
  %%> - the command's vault key name, never the command (R3)
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5140
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  Note over SW: Read the check script<br/>@nckrtl/task-command-assets
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the shipped check script on 209, to send it over SSH in argv
  SW->>N: Check user and directory<br/>@swamp/ssh · getent, test -d
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that the Schedule's user exists on Node 12, with its home, shell and group
  %%> - checks that the user can enter the working directory, for example the Instance's application directory
  %%> - checks the command's hash against the recorded one, with the command on stdin
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the command fails early with a clear error instead of half-running
  SW->>N: Run the command<br/>sudo -u user, timeout, stdin
  %%> How:
  %%> - reads the command with `vault.get` and sends it to Node 12 on stdin, never in argv
  %%> Runs as:
  %%> - the Schedule's user, in its working directory, with its shell
  %%> - production Instances resolve `current` at start, so a new release changes later runs
  %%> Timeout:
  %%> - the Schedule's timeout is the run's time budget
  Note over SW: Report the result<br/>error_code of the failed step
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - prints the public result on 209: ready, or `schedule.target_unavailable`, `schedule.artifact_conflict` or `schedule.run_failed`
  alt exit 0 in time
    N-->>SW: exit 0, output
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the exit code; the output stays in the Swamp step
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result<br/>task succeeded · last run
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5140 succeeds
    %%> - `last_run_at` and `last_run_status` of Schedule 7f3c… are set
  else fails or times out
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - the user or directory is missing, SSH fails, the command exits non-zero, or it runs past its timeout
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>Schedule state unchanged
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5140 fails; the Schedule's last run is marked as an error
    %%> - the Schedule itself and its trigger stay as they were
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - whatever the command changed before it failed stays; Orbit does not roll it back
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run `schedule:run` again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5140
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
